  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J2", "U2", "U3"],
      "pins": ["J2.2", "J2.4", "J2.6", "U2.PB13", "U2.PB14", "U2.PB3"],
      "nets": ["NET0011", "NET0012", "NET0006", "NET0002", "NET0019"],
      "observed": "On the root sheet the Tag-Connect SWD header J2 carries global labels SWDIO (pin 2) = NET0011, SWCLK (pin 4) = NET0012 and SWO (pin 6) = NET0006. The same labels appear on the MCU U2 right-hand column at PB13 (NET0011), PB14 (NET0012) and PB3 (NET0006). Those very PB13/PB14 wires are the ones that run from the DISPLAY sheet symbol (child hierarchical labels NET0002 = DISP_SDA on U3 D1/D2, NET0019 = DISP_SCL on U3 D0). Thus the SWD programming data/clock lines are merged onto the same MCU pins as the OLED I2C bus, and SWO is on PB3 instead of the trace pin.",
      "expected": "The spec states J1's pins are 'VCC, GND, ~{RESET}, SWCLK, SWDIO, SWO - a standard ARM debug/programming interface, not a user I/O', and separately places the display bus on PB12/PB13/PB14 (DISP_SCL on PB13, DISP_SDA on PB14). The SWDIO/SWCLK/SWO lines must connect to the MCU's dedicated debug pins (PA13/PA14/PA15), not to the display I2C pins, so the debug interface and the display bus must remain separate nets.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Root sheet, Programming (SWD): J2 pin 2 -> NET0011, pin 4 -> NET0012, pin 6 -> NET0006 (Conn_ARM_SWD_TagConnect_TC2030)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Root sheet, U2 STM32L476RGT6 right column: red labels NET0011 at PB13, NET0012 at PB14, NET0006 at PB3; the DISPLAY sheet-symbol wires (NET0018/NET0002/NET0019) terminate on PB12/PB13/PB14"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "DISPLAY sheet: U3 D0 (pin 18) -> NET0019, D1/D2 (pins 19/20) -> NET0002"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "DISP_SCL: U2 pin 34 (PB13) ... U4 pin 18 (D0); DISP_SDA: U2 pin 35 (PB14) ... U4 pins 19/20 (D1/D2)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The SWD debug/programming bus and the OLED I2C bus share the same MCU pins (PB13/PB14). Programming the MCU would drive/short the display SDA/SCL lines, and normal display operation would toggle the SWDIO/SWCLK nets, preventing reliable SWD flashing and corrupting the display bus; SWO on PB3 also fails to provide trace output on the expected pin.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic otherwise matches the spec's topology (NFC I2C pull-ups, SSD1306 I2C strapping, TSC series-R + sampling-C, charge-pump network, unresolved antenna cap), but the SWD header's SWDIO/SWCLK/SWO global labels are attached to the MCU pins that carry the OLED I2C bus (PB13/PB14/PB3) instead of the dedicated debug pins, bridging the programming interface with the display bus."
}

